amples, and if applicable, sanity check output predictions are valid and correct.\n\n4. Documentation:\n   - Add a brief docstring at the beginning of the script explaining its purpose\n   - Include additional installation steps with comments at the beginning of the script\n   - Include comments explaining any complex operations or design decisions\n\n5. Others:\n   - To avoid DDP errors, wrap the code in: if __name__ == "__main__":\n   - Ensure errors are propagated up and not silently caught - do not use try/except blocks unless you explicitly re-raise the exception.\n\n6. Validation (only when there is labeled training data):\n   - If there is training and but no validation data is given, hold out a validation dataset (10 percent of the data) at the start, train only on the remaining data.\n   - At the end compute and print the final evaluation metric score on the validation set.\n   - Use a try-except block for the validation step - if validation fails, it\'s acceptable to continue.\n\n\nHyperparameter tuning is unnecessary because the presets is already configured.\nThe usage of document prediction is different from image prediction.\nCheck data path carefully when encounter ValueError: No model is available for this dataset.\nNo need to specify model.names, and do not increase default per gpu batch size to avoid OOM errors.\nFor semantic segmentation, use single GPU by setting CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0\nFor semantic segmentation, the predicted mask should be saved as greyscale JPG image (squeeze then cv2.imwrite) in \\"predicted_mask\\" folder under output folder and save its absolute path in label column.\nAutoGluon MultiModal requires converting multiple label columns into either a single combined label for mutually exclusive cases or training separate predictors for each label in non-exclusive scenarios.\n\n\n\nPlease provide the complete Python script that accomplishes these tasks, ensuring it\'s ready to run given the appropriate data inputs.\n\n### Task Description\n**Task Type:** Image classification (binary classification)\n\n**Input Format:**  \n- Input images of skin lesions (image files; image file names are referenced in the dataset as `image_name`).\n- For prediction, a folder path containing new images.\n\n**Prediction Output Format:**  \n- For each input image, output a malignancy probability (a float between 0 and 1).\n\n**Additional Details:**  \n- The model should classify each lesion as malignant or benign.\n- The evaluation metric is Area Under the Receiver Operator Curve (AUROC).\n- The trained model should be saved.\n- A function must be provided that takes a folder path of new images and returns a malignancy probability for each image.\n\n### Data Structure\nAbsolute path to the folder: /home/anri21/be-fair/mlzero/addition_1_data\n\nFiles structures:\n\n----------\n\nAbsolute path: /home/anri21/be-fair/mlzero/addition_1_data/descriptions.txt\nContent:\nText preview:\nI\'m a dermatologist. My colleagues and I have curated a skin lesion dataset over the past several years. I need a model trained on this data to classify lesions as malignant or benign. The trained model should be saved, and I need a function that takes a folder path of new images and returns a malignancy probability (0–1) for each image. Be fair towards skin tone.\nEvaluation metric: Area under the receiver operator curve (AUROC)\n\n----------\nAbsolute path: /home/anri21/be-fair/mlzero/addition_1_data/mydataset.csv\nContent:\nColumns: [\'Unnamed: 0\', \'super_label\', \'skin_tone\', \'alternative_skin_tone\', \'expert_opinion\', \'label\', \'image_name\']\n Unnamed: 0        super_label  skin_tone  alternative_skin_tone expert_opinion          label image_name\n          0       inflammatory          2                      2            NaN non-neoplastic    image_1\n          1       inflammatory          4                      5            NaN non-neoplastic    image_2\n          2 malignant melanoma          2                      1            NaN      malignant    image_3\n\n----------\n\n\n### User Instruction\n\n\n### Previous Errors\nThese errors were encountered across different implementation approaches and may not be directly related to your current implementation. Use them as reference material to identify potential pitfalls and avoid similar mistakes in your implementation.\n\n\n### Tutorials for Reference\n\n', 'role': 'user'}], 'model': 'gpt-4.1', 'max_completion_tokens': 32768, 'stream': False, 'temperature': 0.1}}
